1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,365
This is a slice through a marine ice sheet, like the one in West Antarctica. The ice rests on bedrock that lies far below sea level.

2
00:00:09,713 --> 00:00:18,822
Where the ice gets thin enough to float, it lifts off the bed. That spot is the grounding line, and this whole story is about where it can sit.

3
00:00:19,146 --> 00:00:22,431
And notice that the bed gets deeper as you go inland.

4
00:00:23,779 --> 00:00:25,806
Why would the slope of the bed matter?

5
00:00:27,146 --> 00:00:40,799
Watch what happens if the grounding line retreats, just a little. The ice there is now thicker, so more ice flows out across it. That thins the ice behind it, which lets the grounding line retreat further still.

6
00:00:41,146 --> 00:00:43,685
A runaway retreat, from a tiny nudge.

7
00:00:44,033 --> 00:00:49,345
This idea was made precise in a 2007 paper by Christian Schoof.

8
00:00:50,700 --> 00:00:59,937
Schoof showed that the flux of ice across the grounding line, q, depends on the ice thickness there, h, raised to a very high power.

9
00:01:00,266 --> 00:01:07,349
With the standard laws for how ice flows and slides, that power is nineteen over four. Almost five.

10
00:01:07,666 --> 00:01:12,295
So doubling the thickness multiplies the outflow by about twenty seven.

11
00:01:13,614 --> 00:01:23,022
Meanwhile, snow falls on the ice upstream. The total input grows with the distance, x g, from the ice divide to the grounding line.

12
00:01:24,348 --> 00:01:29,169
Plot both against grounding line position, with the shape of the bed underneath.

13
00:01:29,502 --> 00:01:35,774
Wherever the two curves cross, input balances output, and the grounding line could sit still.

14
00:01:36,099 --> 00:01:39,939
But look at the middle one. It sits where the bed deepens inland.

15
00:01:40,270 --> 00:01:50,019
Nudge the grounding line back a little. Now the output is bigger than the input, so the ice thins and keeps retreating, all the way to the next crossing.

16
00:01:50,336 --> 00:02:01,451
Steady states can exist where the bed deepens inland, but they are never stable, at least without an ice shelf holding the ice back. That is the marine ice sheet instability.

17
00:02:01,967 --> 00:02:05,615
Thanks for watching. The paper itself is linked below.

